solution_text:
This is a foundational question on Brownian motion. While some aspects like W(0) = 0 may seem trivial, it is essential to explicitly state all defining characteristics and properties.

A continuous-time stochastic process W(t), where t ≥ 0, is called a Brownian motion if it satisfies the following conditions:

• W(0) = 0;

• The increments W(t₁) − W(t₀), W(t₂) − W(t₁), ..., W(tₙ) − W(tₙ₋₁) for any sequence 0 ≤ t₀ < t₁ < ... < tₙ are independent random variables;

• Each increment W(tᵢ₊₁) − W(tᵢ) follows a normal distribution with mean 0 and variance tᵢ₊₁ − tᵢ, i.e., W(tᵢ₊₁) − W(tᵢ) ∼ N(0, tᵢ₊₁ − tᵢ).

Important properties of Brownian motion include:

• Continuous paths (no jumps);

• Zero mean: E[W(t)] = 0;

• Variance equal to time: E[W(t)²] = t;

• Marginal distribution: W(t) ∼ N(0, t);

• Martingale property: E[W(t+s) | W(t)] = W(t);

• Covariance structure: cov(W(s), W(t)) = min(s, t) for 0 < s < t;

• Markov property in continuous space.

Two additional martingales derived from Brownian motion are frequently used in applications:

• Y(t) = W(t)² − t is a martingale.

• Z(t) = exp{α W(t) − ½ α² t}, where α is a constant, is a martingale (exponential martingale).

A proof of the first martingale using Itô’s lemma will be provided in the next section. For the exponential martingale, the key steps are as follows: 2

E[Z(t+s)] = E[exp{α(W(t) + W(s)) − ½ α²(t + s)}]

= exp{−½ α² t} E[exp{α W(t) + α W(s) − ½ α² s}]

= exp{−½ α² t} E[exp{α W(t)}] E[exp{α W(s) − ½ α² s}]

= Z(t) exp{−½ α² t} exp{½ α² t} = Z(t).

B. What is the correlation between a Brownian motion and its square?

Since the array is sorted, any element that differs from its predecessor must be unique. We can iterate through the array and collect elements that are not equal to the previous one. Below is a C++ implementation of this approach:

template <class T>
vector<T> unique(T a[], int n) {
    vector<T> vec; // Vector to store unique elements
    vec.reserve(n); // Pre-allocate memory to avoid reallocation
    vec.push_back(a[0]); // The first element is always unique

    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        if (a[i] != a[i - 1]) { // Check if current element is different from the previous
            vec.push_back(a[i]); // Add to the result if unique
        }
    }

    return vec;
}

This function initializes a vector with the first element of the array and then iterates through the remaining elements. For each element, it compares it with the previous one. If they are different, the element is added to the result vector. The `reserve` call optimizes performance by pre-allocating memory.

Valid Answers: [1, 3, 5, 9]

To compute the moving average efficiently, we leverage the fact that the sum for the next window can be derived from the previous window's sum. Start by calculating the sum of the first n elements to get the initial moving average. For each subsequent element, subtract the element that is sliding out of the window and add the new element that is entering the window. Divide this updated sum by n to obtain the new moving average. The algorithm proceeds as follows:

Initialize the sum S with the first n elements of A and compute B[n] = S / n.
For each index i from n+1 to m:
  Update S by subtracting A[i-n] and adding A[i].
  Compute B[i] = S / n.

Pseudocode for this approach is:
S = A[1] + ... + A[n]; B[n] = S / n;
for (i = n + 1 to m) {
  S = S - A[i - n] + A[i];
  B[i] = S / n;
}

Valid Answers: O(m) time complexity

This algorithm is frequently used in trading systems to compute metrics like maximum run-up or drawdown, making it a common interview topic in quantitative finance contexts.

A straightforward O(n²) approach computes each V(i,j) from scratch using the recurrence:
- V(i,i) = A[i] when i = j
- V(i,j) = V(i,j-1) + A[j] when i < j
During these computations, the algorithm tracks the maximum V(i,j) encountered along with its indices i and j.

A more efficient O(n) solution leverages prefix sums. Define the prefix sum array T where T(k) = Σ_{x=1}^k A[x] with T(0) = 0. Then V(i,j) = T(j) - T(i-1). For any fixed j, maximizing V(i,j) is equivalent to minimizing T(i-1). Thus, the maximum subarray ending at j is Vmax = T(j) - Tmin, where Tmin = min(T(0), T(1), ..., T(j-1)). By maintaining running values of T, Vmax, and Tmin as we iterate through the array, we can compute the solution in linear time.

The following C++ implementation returns the maximum sum Vmax along with the subarray indices i and j:
```cpp
double maxSubarray(double A[], int len, int &i, int &j) {
    double T = A[0], Vmax = A[0];
    double Tmin = std::min(0.0, T);
    i = 0; j = 0;

    for (int k = 1; k < len; ++k) {
        T += A[k];
        if (T - Tmin > Vmax) {
            Vmax = T - Tmin;
            j = k;
        }
        if (T < Tmin) {
            Tmin = T;
            i = (k + 1 < j) ? (k + 1) : j;
        }
    }
    return Vmax;
}
```

Applying this to the array A = {1.0, 2.0, -5.0, 4.0, -3.0, 2.0, 6.0, -5.0, -1.0} yields Vmax = 9.0 with i = 3 and j = 6, corresponding to the subarray [4.0, -3.0, 2.0, 6.0].

problem_text:
The following C++ function computes Fibonacci numbers recursively:

```
int Fibonacci(int n)

if      (n   <= 0)
return 0;
else if (n==1)
return 1;
else
return Fibonacci(n-1)+Fibonacci(n-2);
```

Given that computing `Fibonacci(n)` takes 100 seconds for a large value of `n`, how long (in seconds, rounded to the nearest integer) would it take to compute `Fibonacci(n+1)`? Additionally, is this algorithm efficient, and what alternative methods could be used to compute Fibonacci numbers?
